==[ Round 1] ======
START

Q1.1: After the workshop, what should participants be able to do differently in their day-to-day CSA work? Please describe an observable action rather than a subjective outcome such as “think strategically.”

A1.1: High-leverage work is not about producing a specific implementation. High-leverage work is about defining outcomes, measurable properties, and feedback loops that enable iterative improvement. By running through this exercise, it should provide a tangible demonstration of agentic design with these characteristics defined first (and provide a counter-pattern to adding a bunch of context to an LLM that relies on the model being sufficiently capable and effectively hoping for an outcome)

--
Q1.2: What concrete artifact should each participant leave with after 90 minutes, and what 2–4 observable checks would prove that artifact demonstrates outcome-first agentic design rather than prompt-and-hope?

A1.2: A working demo that also provides value for the user's role. By definition, if you can provide the "checkable finish", bounded-sandbox, and convergent task, then you are working from the outcome first.

--
Q1.3: Which recurring Emerging Technology CSA job should the demo improve first? Choose the highest-priority scenario for candidate ranking.

A1.3: Architecture decision and tradeoff analysis (Recommended)


==[ Round 2] ======
START

Q2.1: What starter package should every participant receive so the 90-minute lab tests loop design rather than setup skill?

A2.1: This is really getting more into the solutioning of the proposed use cases and expect this need to correlate with the scenario. I prefer  scenarios where no starter package is needed (so their loop and system build from zero-to-hero and either time is focused on loop engineering versus time spent learning what tools they were provided by me in the lab) but also recognize some scenarios may be more illustrative with some pre-existing structure. (for these scenarios, consider a small, medium, large level of effort for me to craft the supporting evidence where small is an hour or so to 'vibe' the package, medium is a day or so, and large is anything that will definitely require greater than a day to create)

--
Q2.2 During the lab, what evidence may the loop use to evaluate architecture tradeoffs?

A2.2 Repository-local fictional inputs plus participants’ general expertise; no web or tenant access.

--
Q2.3 Which execution environment should candidate use cases assume for the hands-on lab?

A 2.3 GitHub Copilot CLI or app with repository-local Markdown files only

--

